            0.332,
            0.107
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.6.2 Inclined plane"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.11,
            0.775,
            0.163
          ],
          "content": "Suppose, you want to lift a heavy box onto a platform. Lifting it up vertically (Fig. 7.26a) requires an upward force F equal to its weight. But what if the box is too heavy for you to lift directly?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.17,
            0.422,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.255,
                0.344,
                0.279,
                0.358
              ],
              "content": "(a)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.17,
            0.773,
            0.339
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.598,
                0.344,
                0.622,
                0.358
              ],
              "content": "(b)"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.186,
                0.36,
                0.699,
                0.376
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.26: A box being (a) lifted vertically up, and (b) pushed up the ramp"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.385,
            0.777,
            0.438
          ],
          "content": "You can place the box on a smooth inclined plank as shown in (Fig. 7.26b), and push the box up the plank to the platform. Does this method require a smaller force?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.445,
            0.43,
            0.464
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 7.3: Let us experiment"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.467,
            0.544,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": "1. Take a smooth plank (say a cardboard piece) about 1.5 m long, a toy car (or the cart that you used in Activity 6.3) and a spring balance. Attach the spring balance to the cart. Arrange an elevated surface, such as the top of a low stool or a pile of books, at about 0.5 m height from the floor."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.588,
            0.541,
            0.691
          ],
          "content": "2. First, lift the cart vertically, slowly and steadily, from the floor to the top of the pile of books or stool, and note the reading of the spring balance scale. This reading indicates the force required to lift the cart vertically which is equal to the weight of the cart."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.693,
            0.542,
            0.796
          ],
          "content": "3. Next, place the plank against the top of the pile of books or stool as shown in Fig. 7.27a. Pull the cart along the plank slowly and steadily. Is the reading of the spring balance (the force required) smaller than that of step 2?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.613,
            0.47,
            0.907,
            0.591
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.547,
            0.616,
            0.907,
            0.724
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.714,
                0.596,
                0.738,
                0.611
              ],
              "content": "(a)"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.713,
                0.728,
                0.738,
                0.743
              ],
              "content": "(b)"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.558,
                0.745,
                0.894,
                0.789
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.27: Measuring the force required to pull up a cart along an inclined plank of (a) smaller length, and (b) larger length"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.798,
            0.776,
            0.85
          ],
          "content": "4. Now, reduce the angle between the plank and the base as shown in Fig. 7.27b, and repeat the step 3. Observe how the force required changes as the plank becomes less steep."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.852,
            0.776,
            0.906
          ],
          "content": "The force required to pull up the cart upto the height of the pile of books or stool decreases as the plank becomes less steep. However, you have to apply the force over a larger distance to bring it to the same height."
        }
      ]
    }
